{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-mixed-demo-review", "file": "references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md", "content": "# Seed-motif generator checkpoint after a wrong submission

A salient colored seed embedded in a noisy grid does not by itself imply a copy rule. Candidate classes can include translated shape occurrences, periodic/lattice replication, directional rays, or combinations. Before extrapolating, record the seed's exact coordinates and the complete input/output difference mask in every demo. Verify both positive and negative cases: why each changed cell is selected, and why visually similar unchanged cells are excluded. Check palettes and dimensions that vary across demos; distinguish the seed color, target/background color, and unrelated noise empirically rather than assuming one role from a single pair.

After an incorrect submission, discard the whole generator. Do not move a few cells, swap a presumed background color, or switch to a nearby geometric interpretation without replaying it against every demo. With a costly attempt budget, use remaining demos for explicit competing hypotheses and only submit after a full-mask validation. Preserve all cells outside the supported transformation and verify dimensions/row lengths.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-mixed-demo-review/references/seed-motif-generator-checkpoint.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/diff-mask-lesson.md", "content": "# Exact diff masks for motif/propagation hypotheses

A conspicuous seed motif does not prove that every translated match should be painted. For each demonstration, compute the complete input/output diff as a set of coordinates and classify each changed cell by its original value. Independently enumerate predicted changes for candidate mechanisms (mask translations, rays, periodic propagation, or other spatial rules), including any eligibility condition such as requiring a particular background value.

Validate exact set equality: every changed demo cell must be predicted, and every predicted cell must actually change. Record unchanged lookalikes and existing motif cells as negative evidence. Keep generator, eligible input values, and selection scope separate; after an incorrect submission reject the complete generator rather than making a cosmetic adjustment. Apply the surviving rule to the test only after it fits all demos. This is especially important when demos vary in palette, seed geometry, or grid dimensions.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792544451/attempts/07c41a7601b34a5db818bb4c02b598f2/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-solving-continual/references/diff-mask-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-solving-continual", "file": "references/expansion-after-error.md", "content": "# Expansion inference after an incorrect tiling prediction

When output dimensions are a multiple of input dimensions, do not assume the input is repeated into every block. A tiny example (such as a 1×1 input expanded to 2×2) is compatible with many generators and does not establish how larger inputs expand. For every larger demo, compare each output block separately with the input, its rotations/reflections, and other plausible transforms. Record which cells are preserved and which are newly generated; do not label irregular-looking cells as random noise without testing a common rule.

